}

abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "The research domain is mesoscale biology, specifically the study of biomolecular condensates formed by liquid-liquid phase separation and their emergent material properties. The primary scientific gap is the lack of an integrated, predictive framework linking sequence-encoded biophysics to emergent mesoscale material properties and downstream cellular phenotypes. The primary objective is to identify the sequence-encoded biophysical rules governing the material properties of condensates by harmonizing cross-database resources, training interpretable machine learning models, and mapping disease-associated mutations to predicted property shifts and cellular vulnerabilities. Fragmented biophysical, structural, imaging, multi-omics, and clinical datasets will be integrated into a harmonized relational database anchored by UniProt accessions, using automated ID-mapping pipelines, conflict-resolution logic, and a standardized ontology for condensate-relevant features. The research is significant because it synthesizes multidisciplinary data to test if generalizable sequence-to-material-property rules exist, enabling the determination of how perturbations to these rules affect cellular and organismal phenotypes."
}

