abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "The research domain encompasses biomolecular condensates, specifically how molecular interactions drive emergent mesoscale material properties such as liquid-liquid phase separation. A critical scientific gap is the lack of a unified, quantitative framework linking molecular grammar to these properties, primarily due to fragmented and unstandardized data silos across diverse sequence, structural, and biophysical datasets. The objective of this study is to construct an interoperable meta-database, CondensateAtlas, and apply a machine-learning framework to predict condensate material behavior from primary sequence and cellular context, enabling the distinction between functional liquids and pathological solid aggregates. To achieve this, multi-scale data from numerous repositories, including PhaSePro, LLPSDB, and ClinVar, will be standardized via ontologies and natural language processing before being integrated into a relational and graph-hybrid database linking sequence records, interactome metrics, and material-property annotations. Establishing this predictive molecular grammar will significantly improve the interpretation of disease variants in neurodegeneration and cancer, aid in the design of novel therapeutics, and support advancements in synthetic condensate engineering."
}

