}

abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "The domain of mesoscale biology focuses on the emergence of biomolecular condensates formed via liquid-liquid phase separation. A critical scientific gap exists in the lack of an integrated framework connecting molecular sequence grammar to emergent mesoscale condensate behavior and cellular phenotypes due to fragmented, incompatible data resources. The primary objective is to synthesize public databases into a unified machine-learning framework to predict condensate participation, material properties, and the effects of disease-associated mutations. Diverse sequence, structural, imaging, and clinical datasets will be integrated by mapping universal protein accessions, resolving identifier mismatches, and merging multi-modal data into a composite weighted graph and unified relational schema. This research is significant because it provides a generalizable analytical template linking molecular grammar to network topology and mesoscale phenotypes, enabling predictions of how specific perturbations drive pathological condensate behavior."
}

