abstract_text:
[
  "The research domain of mesoscale cellular biology focuses on the emergent material properties of biomolecular condensates formed via liquid-liquid phase separation. A scientific gap exists regarding how the combinatorial rules of sequence composition, structural motifs, post-translational modifications, and interaction networks dictate these material properties and subsequent cellular functions, largely because existing datasets remain siloed. The primary objective is to apply machine learning methods, including graph neural networks and gradient-boosted trees, to predict condensate material properties from molecular features and link them to cellular outcomes. To achieve this, disparate public databases will be synthesized into a unified relational meta-database using a controlled-vocabulary ontology, integrated with standardized image-analysis pipelines designed to extract material-property proxies from imaging data. This research will establish a generalizable framework for mesoscale emergence, explain functional divergence in chemically similar condensates, and provide predictive models to guide biomolecular condensate engineering."
]

