abstract_text:
[
  "Biomolecular condensates form via liquid-liquid phase separation, guided by a sequence-encoded molecular grammar that produces emergent material properties and cellular functions. Despite abundant multi-scale data, a critical scientific gap remains in the lack of an integrated predictive framework connecting molecular sequences and biophysical parameters to cellular outcomes and disease phenotypes. The primary objective is to synthesize fragmented molecular data using biophysical polymer models and ensemble machine learning to predict the emergent properties of condensates and determine if deviations reveal disease mechanisms. The data integration strategy involves harmonizing diverse heterogenous datasets—spanning sequence features, interactomes, imaging, and transcriptomics—by mapping them to a collaboratively developed standardized ontology within a unified relational meta-database. This framework will decode the fundamental sequence-to-phenotype rules of condensate emergence and provide actionable insights into neurodegenerative diseases and cancers linked to aberrant phase separation."
]

