]

abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "The research domain of biomolecular condensate biology focuses on liquid-liquid phase separation and the emergent mesoscale properties of membraneless organelles. However, an extensive volume of heterogeneous data remains siloed across disciplines, preventing a unified understanding of how sequence and structural features dictate emergent mesoscale organization and downstream cellular phenotypes. The primary objective is to identify a generalizable molecular grammar that predicts condensate phase separation propensity, material state transitions, and causal links to cellular phenotypes by training multi-modal graph neural networks and transformer-based sequence models. Diverse public datasets will be integrated by developing standardized vocabularies to reconcile inconsistent nomenclature, merging sequence features with structural embeddings into a unified graph representation where nodes represent proteins and edges represent interactions. Discovering these rules will provide a generalizable framework for studying biological emergence across systems, which is critical because dysregulated condensate properties are implicated in neurodegenerative diseases, cancer, and viral infections."
}

