}

abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "The research domain centers on mesoscale emergence in cell biology, specifically focusing on biomolecular condensates formed via liquid-liquid phase separation. Currently, there is no predictive, generalizable framework that links molecular determinants and cellular context to emergent mesoscale material behavior and downstream cellular or disease phenotypes. The primary objective is to determine if synthesizing existing public molecular, structural, cellular, and clinical data using machine learning models can predict protein condensate formation, material properties, and the pathological impacts of disease-associated mutations. These fragmented datasets will be integrated by developing a shared ontology with UniProt-anchored protein identifiers and engineering standardized feature representations across domains. By building the first cross-scale model of condensate emergence using entirely public data, this study will establish generalizable principles of mesoscale self-organization and identify mechanisms of condensate dysregulation in diseases."
}

