abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "The research domain of mesoscale emergence focuses on biomolecular condensates formed via liquid-liquid phase separation. A critical scientific gap remains in establishing a unified understanding that links sequence features, structural motifs, and network topologies to causal effects on condensate material properties and cellular outcomes because current structural, network, and phenotypic datasets are fragmented and incompatible. The primary objective is to conduct a secondary data analysis utilizing automated imaging feature extraction, network analysis, and machine learning to synthesize existing public datasets into an integrated multiscale atlas that determines the features governing condensate properties and disease susceptibility. Data from diverse public repositories will be integrated into a relational database by employing UniProt accessions as a central harmonization backbone, mapping a newly developed controlled vocabulary, and applying computer-vision pipelines to overcome heterogeneous reporting formats. This research will establish the first cross-system, validated framework linking molecular composition to mesoscale behavior, providing a reusable template for integrating data on other multi-scale cellular phenomena."
}

