abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "The research domain of cellular biology and biophysics currently focuses on biomolecular condensates formed through liquid-liquid phase separation and their emergent mesoscale material properties. However, a scientific gap remains due to the lack of a unified predictive framework connecting the molecular sequence and structural features of condensates to their mesoscale material properties and resulting cellular or disease phenotypes. The primary objective is to determine how sequence-encoded molecular grammar dictates condensate material properties and propagates to cellular and disease phenotypes by utilizing machine learning and biophysical models. To support this approach, isolated datasets spanning sequence predictors, cellular imaging, and clinical variants will be integrated into a FAIR-compliant relational meta-database using a standardized identifier crosswalk and controlled vocabularies. Developing this validated predictive framework will transition condensate biology from a descriptive to a predictive science, thereby enabling rational therapeutic design and providing a model for biological emergence."
}

