}

abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "Mesoscale cell biology currently focuses on the formation and properties of biomolecular condensates via liquid-liquid phase separation. However, there is a lack of a predictive framework linking the sequence features of condensate-forming proteins to their emergent mesoscale material properties, cellular functions, and disease-related phenotypes because existing multi-scale data resources remain siloed and utilize incompatible metrics. The primary objective is to determine the sequence-encoded and network-level features that predict the emergent material and functional properties of condensates across scales, utilizing machine learning algorithms and image-analysis tools to evaluate how disease mutations perturb this mapping. Diverse databases spanning biophysical, imaging, and genomic data will be integrated using a computational harmonization pipeline to resolve identifier mismatches, standardize sequence features, and systematically link metadata. Ultimately, this research will provide the first cross-scale, multi-database framework for mesoscale emergence, enabling the prediction of novel variant pathogenicity and guiding the design of condensate-modulating therapeutics."
}

