{
  "abstract": "The research domain of mesoscale biology focuses on the emergent behavior, formation, and function of biomolecular condensates, which are driven by molecular properties such as intrinsic disorder and multivalent interaction motifs. However, these features alone are insufficient to predict phenotypic outcomes, leaving it unknown why only certain phase-separating molecules form functional cellular condensates while others do not, and what causes these condensates to become pathological. The primary objective is to develop a data-driven synthesis framework by constructing a multimodal knowledge graph from existing public datasets and analyzing it with machine learning and causal inference models to identify the features predicting condensate membership and behavior. Diverse genomic, proteomic, and imaging datasets will be integrated into this versioned knowledge graph using stable identifiers, ontology mappings, evidence codes, and confidence scores to link nodes and edges across different modalities. Synthesizing these fragmented, single-modality datasets into an extensible framework will provide mechanistic insights into how molecular interactions create mesoscale organization and influence cellular function and disease."
