]

abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "Within cellular biology and biophysics, biomolecular condensates and membraneless organelles emerge via liquid-liquid phase separation. However, it remains unknown how nanoscale molecular properties, sequences, and structures dictate emergent mesoscale material properties and cellular phenotypes due to the fragmentation of existing data. The objective of this computational synthesis project is to determine how molecular grammar dictates condensate architecture and predict material states using physics-informed Graph Neural Networks. To support this approach, heterogeneous molecular, structural, interactomic, and imaging datasets will be integrated via automated harmonization pipelines into a unified multi-relational knowledge graph. Understanding these phase transitions will significantly contribute to explaining the pathogenesis of neurodegenerative diseases and cancers, ultimately guiding the rational design of synthetic condensates and targeted therapeutics."
}

rephrased_abstract:
{
  "abstract": "Within cellular biology and biophysics, biomolecular condensates and membraneless organelles emerge via liquid-liquid phase separation. However, it remains unknown how nanoscale molecular properties, sequences, and structures dictate emergent mesoscale material properties and cellular phenotypes due to the fragmentation of existing data. The objective of this computational synthesis project is to determine how molecular grammar dictates condensate architecture and predict material states using physics-informed Graph Neural Networks. To support this approach, heterogeneous molecular, structural, interactomic, and imaging datasets will be integrated via automated harmonization pipelines into a unified multi-relational knowledge graph. Understanding these phase transitions will significantly contribute to explaining the pathogenesis of neurodegenerative diseases and cancers, ultimately guiding the rational design of synthetic condensates and targeted therapeutics."
}

abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "In molecular and cellular biology, the emergence of mesoscale biomolecular condensates formed via liquid-liquid phase separation represents a critical focus of study. Despite the availability of extensive multimodal datasets spanning sequence, structure, biophysics, interactomics, and imaging, these resources remain highly siloed, leaving the principles dictating how one-dimensional sequence features and local physical interactions scale into complex three-dimensional mesoscale behaviors unknown. The primary objective is to employ a computational data synthesis strategy utilizing multimodal artificial intelligence, including Graph Neural Networks and spatio-temporal deep learning, to uncover the rules governing biomolecular condensate emergence and their impact on cellular fitness. Data from repositories such as DisProt, UniProt, AlphaFold DB, PhaSePro, CD-CODE, BioGRID, PRIDE, and the Image Data Resource will be integrated by developing a unified graph-based ontology to create standardized matrices aligned by protein identifiers and cellular conditions, and synthesized using latent space alignment. This multimodal synthesis is expected to elucidate the deterministic versus stochastic nature of cellular organization and inform the rational design of synthetic condensates."
}

