abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "Mesoscale cellular organization focuses on the emergence of biomolecular condensates formed via the liquid-liquid phase separation of intrinsically disordered proteins and nucleic acids. A critical scientific gap exists because fragmented experimental data prevents a unified, systems-level understanding of how nanometer-scale interactions collectively drive the emergence of micrometer-scale mesoscopic phenotypes. The primary objective is to decode the physicochemical rules governing condensate emergence by employing multi-modal graph neural networks, deep representation learning, and 3D convolutional neural networks to predict biophysical properties from molecular networks. To achieve this, an automated data pipeline will integrate over 50,000 public records from DisProt, PRIDE, the AlphaFold Protein Structure Database, and the BioImage Archive into a unified multi-scale knowledge graph that maps relationships between primary sequences, 3D structural motifs, physical interaction networks, and spatial phenotypes. This work will establish an artificial intelligence-driven analytical framework for mesoscale biology, enabling the rational design of synthetic condensates and providing insights into cellular stress resilience."
}

