abstract_text:
[
  "Biological emergence at the mesoscale involves the formation of membraneless organelles via liquid-liquid phase separation. A critical gap remains in understanding how molecular sequence grammar and dynamic cellular microenvironments integrate to determine condensate material properties, and how these transitions fail to form irreversible, aberrant aggregates. The primary objective is to decode the rules governing the mesoscale emergence of biomolecular condensates and their impact on cellular stress resilience by synthesizing existing multiscale datasets through community-scale harmonization, AI-driven modeling, and iterative transdisciplinary synthesis. Siloed information from databases such as the Protein Data Bank, DisProt, PRIDE, BioGRID, the Image Data Resource, EMPIAR, and public metabolomics datasets will be integrated by developing a standardized ontology, utilizing automated metadata extraction, and employing semantic web technologies to map molecular features to cellular imaging metrics. Ultimately, this research will shift the field from descriptive phenomenology to the quantitative prediction of pathological condensate transitions associated with neurodegenerative diseases such as ALS and Alzheimer's."
]

rephrased_abstract:
[
  "Biological emergence at the mesoscale involves the formation of membraneless organelles via liquid-liquid phase separation. A critical gap remains in understanding how molecular sequence grammar and dynamic cellular microenvironments integrate to determine condensate material properties, and how these transitions fail to form irreversible, aberrant aggregates. The primary objective is to decode the rules governing the mesoscale emergence of biomolecular condensates and their impact on cellular stress resilience by synthesizing existing multiscale datasets through community-scale harmonization, AI-driven modeling, and iterative transdisciplinary synthesis. Siloed information from databases such as the Protein Data Bank, DisProt, PRIDE, BioGRID, the Image Data Resource, EMPIAR, and public metabolomics datasets will be integrated by developing a standardized ontology, utilizing automated metadata extraction, and employing semantic web technologies to map molecular features to cellular imaging metrics. Ultimately, this research will shift the field from descriptive phenomenology to the quantitative prediction of pathological condensate transitions associated with neurodegenerative diseases such as ALS and Alzheimer's."
